#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115072 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "239ee94c-c3d9-5955-8cdd-afff77ec3279" :question/math-academy-id "e-4716" :question/problem "Is \n$\\begin{aligned}E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$ \nan elementary matrix?" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation.\n\nThe given matrix $E$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I_2$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_2:= R_2+(-5) R_1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115073 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c56b3e9-cf23-5e1e-8c7d-c474059c9a9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "40cdd39f-29c3-5dad-9708-02c3e0de6acb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4718" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n![](images/03/03d58fbe64af980e170a695b5f511df7e14cd74cc894e802ce5d781642d58b2b.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We write down $E$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n![](images/7b/7bb507644e3602983ed613dbe3b630fbd4fe72799c5412272a072a39c736cbc5.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115074 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd12bc7b-1b5d-5af2-bcef-c9d0fda7d8fc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Product Involving the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "ed71f469-f0e9-5016-846d-cb70d59ab3ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-4719" :question/problem "Find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1},$ where ![](images/c2/c26c31fb0c03ed31508d71c394a0cac97706b8e98f495758ef1bc3cb7b9de37f.svg) and ![](images/27/2728c629af340140b991c75f58104833304f4dc82d6f2f04b29b6ddde363b349.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $E_1$ and $E_2$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find $E_2^{-1}.$ We write down $E_2$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n![](images/11/11f12b427f63eb036d2f19210ab85c0a5b279bfda547031e8ae867d130aafc15.svg)\n\nNow, we can find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/4c/4c46704dbedb6bb10fd40107493f49481923b4f1e524dc8f3f84d98aa4f66256.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69657e5e-ddb2-5905-87d9-718781e63186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400501} {:answer/id #uuid "85f6e2e5-5000-5e4e-823a-50db2fc79262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400502} {:answer/id #uuid "375a7515-6b30-5def-bfb9-8102113b1869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400503} {:answer/id #uuid "4d61a059-dddb-5e13-801e-a014bc95f9c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400504} {:answer/id #uuid "d61776a1-23a9-569a-9478-5925bcddef45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69657e5e-ddb2-5905-87d9-718781e63186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400501} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce429fdf-6b6f-5359-bf73-1a833c32c68d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d49dd3c-2d26-5daf-baa6-13e98e158d10" :question/math-academy-id "q-130772" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrices $P$ and $Q$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $S$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =S\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $S$ is an elementary matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1494e45b-e9c6-535d-9eba-3790fc3b3525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400508} {:answer/id #uuid "9c2aeafd-d65d-5e22-bb2e-c7f378129297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400509} {:answer/id #uuid "15a62809-c816-544c-b95c-2f003c843342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$  and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400510} {:answer/id #uuid "97c97b06-f6db-546d-aa97-0f3a35c8b667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400511} {:answer/id #uuid "164d8bbe-3777-525f-8440-8bfe402f43f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$  and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97c97b06-f6db-546d-aa97-0f3a35c8b667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400511} :answer-field/id #uuid "70925ade-385f-5362-ac6e-a022a4d19eae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea3b1565-0857-5d0a-8afe-805754b2cf2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-130778" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrices $A$ and $C$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=4{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:=4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is an elementary matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c56b3e9-cf23-5e1e-8c7d-c474059c9a9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ef815bd-f246-5489-a173-af59955aa440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388162} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b90574-37d3-5939-9d6d-f41a132e44c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{7} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388163} {:answer/id #uuid "e0797fd8-3780-510c-a616-159309484a73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{7} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388164} {:answer/id #uuid "dd6a68cc-a941-50cf-87a0-dbba7667c8dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388165} {:answer/id #uuid "2f63056e-6d68-5d2d-a877-8755ac4c2845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0797fd8-3780-510c-a616-159309484a73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{7} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388164} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e98b29a-38f8-524d-ac20-5c631c095e5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6550d7b1-7dc0-5855-b6b5-da0256aaa98e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130798" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $Q$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Q\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{7} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{7}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{7} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{Q}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{7} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c56b3e9-cf23-5e1e-8c7d-c474059c9a9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b54b601-afa4-54fb-9b81-8e1a343fbbd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436484} {:answer/id #uuid "722609aa-a0d5-5e3b-9591-7ee5f0d9b3af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436490} {:answer/id #uuid "74fbade3-24cf-5469-a14c-c1b53476a0da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436491} {:answer/id #uuid "a943f519-fb72-5834-9e86-9b373dc13537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436500} {:answer/id #uuid "afe75d39-2b1f-5943-9262-25e98dd52004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b54b601-afa4-54fb-9b81-8e1a343fbbd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436484} :answer-field/id #uuid "57d2ad5f-fd97-5e2d-883f-36f12c7f88c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "682600e4-1f6c-58ba-ae45-caa8f795950e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130800" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $V$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[W\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{6} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{W}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd12bc7b-1b5d-5af2-bcef-c9d0fda7d8fc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Product Involving the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0449b553-1b65-5cac-8598-b0d8a270a950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}21 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263039} {:answer/id #uuid "08cf208e-4a67-56fb-af8e-a3fd93304b09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263084} {:answer/id #uuid "79e21f99-e8cc-51c3-a318-a7ddd869b864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264057} {:answer/id #uuid "7e60b2a6-6056-5ea2-8a17-c6be4b8de34e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}19 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264091} {:answer/id #uuid "9e4de5ce-9af6-520d-b7aa-ae9f2db45567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 & -7 \\\\ 8 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0449b553-1b65-5cac-8598-b0d8a270a950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}21 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263039} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcf3b9f2-af48-548d-9af3-5530034cb7ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8b759c1-7acc-5a06-82c3-b179ddb571b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-130823" :question/problem "Find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}21 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd12bc7b-1b5d-5af2-bcef-c9d0fda7d8fc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Product Involving the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "111040c6-f52f-5fe4-8365-864262986013" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436477} {:answer/id #uuid "2eb70ba9-1167-5fc0-a5c7-cfac02437eab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436481} {:answer/id #uuid "8d99ac86-82e5-5e0a-9493-269c69c2ba02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436495} {:answer/id #uuid "bcbeb476-106e-56ab-bc8d-5ea75f68ee56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436504} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f4c4eb-37ab-559c-af8f-9eacddcde995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d99ac86-82e5-5e0a-9493-269c69c2ba02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436495} :answer-field/id #uuid "a181bc03-12d6-5f9e-af68-66c71236d0b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2f3128b-e14c-5d17-b8ee-71ffa4c47e70" :question/math-academy-id "q-130825" :question/problem "Find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3156da43-688a-5c56-bb8a-3fd05e02f8ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$F$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436482} {:answer/id #uuid "5ca02a73-7d89-56f2-b95b-c2a98081117a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$E$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436486} {:answer/id #uuid "781bf9fc-c8ed-589b-bc49-9fe5c4bb51ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ and $E$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436492} {:answer/id #uuid "c42dc014-434e-5a33-be09-1ba19122dbc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436507} {:answer/id #uuid "c82ee549-4efd-554c-bd79-6314d62f5a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$E$ and $F$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ca02a73-7d89-56f2-b95b-c2a98081117a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$E$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436486} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a39083d-7da8-5ae0-912f-f708e52fb22e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73b54284-6d5f-5b76-a388-21fc0778bb8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-31889" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,F=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $D$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $E$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $F$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $E$ is an elementary matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd12bc7b-1b5d-5af2-bcef-c9d0fda7d8fc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Product Involving the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bfc713a-679b-5cfa-9d6f-08a36b86fb51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436496} {:answer/id #uuid "a74234e5-d97b-566c-bed3-339abfaef5b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -11 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436498} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f73949-1a41-5465-a743-a686174de006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436499} {:answer/id #uuid "c0bb3be0-28be-55c7-a77b-ae02e4febeba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436506} {:answer/id #uuid "e25a7759-8c03-50b5-a54a-8024f5ffd9a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-11 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bfc713a-679b-5cfa-9d6f-08a36b86fb51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436496} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed86b220-5180-5369-9230-c6b72b1d1579" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1814a01e-7b9b-5bac-ab6c-86bbec04e6cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-31932" :question/problem "Find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18d70a21-a0a1-52b7-a045-c0e37700fe18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113972} {:answer/id #uuid "28fc5d52-09e8-57ce-bd4d-7baffb2ecb7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113973} {:answer/id #uuid "2c1b7c76-6628-5d16-9a54-e87abbc2a70e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113974} {:answer/id #uuid "44214a93-d7f3-5f9e-b2dd-0413f02116b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113975} {:answer/id #uuid "518d2199-bb64-5f22-b1be-c70f50c7109c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28fc5d52-09e8-57ce-bd4d-7baffb2ecb7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113973} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fa66a0f-b04d-553e-85a2-176076113954" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60c27a10-b6b0-5a54-9d51-0c91bf9fbc6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-42591" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $P$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =P\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrices $Q$ and $S$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $P$ is an elementary matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c56b3e9-cf23-5e1e-8c7d-c474059c9a9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34da3084-a21c-5f11-a32e-01588ddf5fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436483} {:answer/id #uuid "bba143df-3184-57ce-bc93-47ed7a3522c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436503} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd5f231-30de-5f93-92ba-901fd84afa6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436505} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c585a9-2f07-5403-bcb1-dabce9f67e3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436508} {:answer/id #uuid "e5167b06-7c0b-5f26-8c68-4d6d734b259b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcd5f231-30de-5f93-92ba-901fd84afa6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436505} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3e2b631-2958-5e53-a350-a255155a7da2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0ff6849-42fb-5905-961f-eb146b05e241" :question/math-academy-id "q-42697" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436529 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "081decf6-8b8b-5b4e-bc55-884af37deef0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436474} {:answer/id #uuid "58355426-69d0-55ca-a705-4a9d72d17bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$N$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436485} {:answer/id #uuid "609661d9-134c-5281-8a45-969f241f92a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436487} {:answer/id #uuid "7863b914-5ce1-519d-8bdf-4c54739802f9"